{"name":"Byzantine Copper","hex":"#ab7141","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"byzantine","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Alternative letter-case form of Byzantine ."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A byzant (coin)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/byzantine"},{"term":"copper","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A reddish-brown metallic chemical element (symbol Cu) with the atomic number 29; also, the metal made up of this element.","The reddish-brown colour of copper (etymology 1, noun sense 1)."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made of copper (etymology 1, noun sense 1).","Having the reddish-brown colour of copper."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/copper"}],"entries":[{"title":"Byzantine Empire","blocks":[{"text":"The Byzantine Empire, also known as the Eastern Roman Empire, was the continuation of the Eastern part of the Roman Empire centred on Constantinople during late antiquity and the Middle Ages. Having survived the fall of the Western Roman Empire in the 5th century AD, it endured until the fall of Constantinople to the Ottoman Empire in 1453. The term 'Byzantine Empire' was coined only after its demise; its citizens used the term 'Roman Empire' and called themselves 'Romans'."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Eastern_Roman_Empire_565_CE.svg/330px-Eastern_Roman_Empire_565_CE.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Byzantine_Empire","wikidata":"Q12544"},{"title":"Copper","blocks":[{"text":"Copper is a chemical element; it has symbol Cu and atomic number 29. It is a soft, malleable, and ductile metal with very high thermal and electrical conductivity. A freshly exposed surface of pure copper has a pinkish-orange color. Copper is used as a conductor of heat and electricity, as a building material, and as a constituent of various metal alloys, such as sterling silver used in jewelry, cupronickel used to make marine hardware and coins, and constantan used in strain gauges and thermocouples for temperature measurement."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/NatCopper.jpg/330px-NatCopper.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Copper","wikidata":"Q753"},{"title":"Copper (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Copper is a reddish brown color that resembles the metal copper. The first recorded use of copper as a color name in English was in 1594."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Copper-18706.jpg/330px-Copper-18706.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Copper_(color)","wikidata":"Q2722041"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Statua_di_Costantino_ai_musei_capitolini.jpg/500px-Statua_di_Costantino_ai_musei_capitolini.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Statua_di_Costantino_ai_musei_capitolini.jpg/1280px-Statua_di_Costantino_ai_musei_capitolini.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Statua_di_Costantino_ai_musei_capitolini.jpg/500px-Statua_di_Costantino_ai_musei_capitolini.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Constantine I, also known as Constantine the Great, was Roman emperor from AD 306 to 337 and the first Roman emperor to convert to Christianity. He founded the city of Constantinople (now Istanbul) and made it the eastern capital of the Empire, which it remained for over a millennium.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AStatua_di_Costantino_ai_musei_capitolini.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Mosaic_o